{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DQ8KFS8uUWVq2jyFencHtC","type":"tool_result","content":"['E05', 'E06', 'E07', 'E08', 'E09']\nE05 Verified, namespaced tools Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.\n  E05.1 Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name. | None\n  E05.2 The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers. | ['repeated_confusables', 'multiple_edits', 'normalization_boundary']\n  E05.3 Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool. | None\nE06 Enforced gateway boundary Send MCP calls through a gateway or equivalent enforcement layer the agent cannot bypass. Choose controls for internal HTTP servers, public third-party services, and local stdio servers; private networking and mTLS are one internal deployment pattern, not universal MCP requirements. Authenticate each caller, preserve agent and delegated-subject identity, and authorize each action's tool, operation, resource, tenant, and arguments. Apply the same policy to direct calls, dynamic tools, CLI/code mode, and wrappers. Validate calls and responses, enforce rate limits, and log every decision. For HTTP OAuth, validate access tokens for the gateway's own audience, issuer, expiry, and scopes; obtain separately authorized backend credentials through a trusted issuer or supported exchange. Never accept a token for another resource or relay it unchanged as a substitute for backend authorization. Keep backend secrets and tokens away from agent-controlled execution paths. Restrict local process launch, files, environment, credentials, and network in the host or sandbox. Under MCP 2026-07-28, reject metadata-header/body mismatches before routing or policy relies on them. Isolate private caches by verified authorization context, tenant, resource, and policy version; share only data explicitly approved as public. Authorize explicit task/state handles on every operation; neither a connection nor self-reported metadata establishes identity. Where the gateway is an OAuth client, validate authorization-server issuer responses and protect discovery URLs against SSRF. Record protocol and extension versions and test each enabled extension's boundary, including task access/cancellation and MCP Apps sandbox, CSP, bridge messages, and UI-initiated tool authorization. Test bypass, cross-tenant access, malformed traffic, and required-check outages. Required checks fail closed.\n  E06.1 Every governed tool path uses an enforced boundary suited to its internal HTTP, public third-party, or local stdio deployment; agent-controlled code cannot bypass it or obtain backend credentials. | None\n  E06.2 The gateway validates its own incoming token audience, issuer, expiry and scopes, and obtains separately authorized, backend-specific credentials while preserving agent and delegated-subject identity. | None\n  E06.3 Every direct, dynamic, CLI/code-mode and wrapper call receives action/resource/tenant/argument authorization, request/response validation, rate limits and decision logging. | None\n  E06.4 If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked. | None\n  E06.5 Under MCP 2026-07-28, relevant header/body mismatches are rejected before routing or policy relies on them, and private caches/state/task handles are isolated by verified authorization context, tenant, resource and policy version. | None\n  E06.6 If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client. | None\n  E06.7 Enabled MCP extensions have recorded versions and enforced boundaries, including Tasks ownership/cancellation and Apps sandbox/CSP/bridge/tool-call authorization where present. | None\nE07 Untrusted peer messages Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.\n  E07.1 Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed. | None\n  E07.2 Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant. | None\n  E07.3 Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects. | None\n  E07.4 Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts. | None\n  E07.5 Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked. | None\nE08 Bounded delegated authority Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Preserve the verified original subject and current actor through each handoff and enforce their task, tenant, resource, and audience limits where the action runs. Use supported token exchange or narrow task credentials rather than copying a parent's full credential. A parent must not gain a denied power by asking a more powerful peer to act without separate valid approval. For RFC 8693 tokens, earlier nested actors are provenance only and never add access. An actor chain or signed identity does not prove that a requested action was approved. Test legitimate delegation and that escalation attempt.\n  E08.1 Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential. | None\n  E08.2 Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access. | None\n  E08.3 An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval. | None\n  E08.4 Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens). | None\nE09 Recursive shutdown Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.\n  E09.1 A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. | None\n  E09.2 Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit. | None\n  E09.3 Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree. | None\n  E09.4 Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation. | None\nScanned 36 files with 52 patterns: 11 hits written to ../agent/brace-prescan.json\n  B02: 1\n  B11: 4\n  E03: 1