{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019jfyPAT2ngcPi2nMkZRAuW","type":"tool_result","content":"Added red flag to E06.\nAdded red flag to E06.\nAdded red flag to E06.\nSaved item E06 = gap. Next: E07.1 — Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n  How to verify: 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\nSaved E07.1 = no. Next: E07.2 — Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n  How to verify: 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\nSaved E07.2 = no. Next: E07.3 — Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n  How to verify: 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\nSaved E07.3 = no. Next: E07.4 — Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\nSaved E07.4 = no. Next: E07.5 — Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n  How to verify: 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\nSaved E07.5 = no. Next: finish item E07 with `answer.py ... item E07 --rating ...`\nAdded red flag to E07.\nAdded red flag to E07.\nSaved item E07 = gap. Next: E08.1 — Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0de488e7-937f-49f5-af13-02cd18e903c1","uuid":"3017ff7d-c9ac-434c-85c3-dd87f60fb0dc","timestamp":"2026-10-20T15:54:58.709Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Added red flag to E06.\nAdded red flag to E06.\nAdded red flag to E06.\nSaved item E06 = gap. Next: E07.1 — Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n  How to verify: 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.\nSaved E07.1 = no. Next: E07.2 — Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.\n  How to verify: 1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body.\nSaved E07.2 = no. Next: E07.3 — Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.\n  How to verify: 1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects.\nSaved E07.3 = no. Next: E07.4 — Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template.\nSaved E07.4 = no. Next: E07.5 — Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.\n  How to verify: 1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address.\nSaved E07.5 = no. Next: finish item E07 with `answer.py ... item E07 --rating ...`\nAdded red flag to E07.\nAdded red flag to E07.\nSaved item E07 = gap. Next: E08.1 — Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction